                       "id":  "ingles-38",
                       "texto_apoio":  "Researchers studying urban noise have found that residents living near busy highways show consistently higher levels of cortisol, a hormone linked to stress, than those in quieter neighborhoods. Interestingly, the same studies found that simply adding vegetation barriers between roads and residential buildings reduced both perceived noise and measured cortisol levels, even when actual decibel levels changed only slightly.",
